{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
  },
  "AllowedHosts": "*",

  // #52. Present and empty, which is a configured state and not an oversight.
  // AuthenticationSetup reads this key to decide who may create an account: with
  // it blank, registration on a developer machine asks for no code, and anywhere
  // else it refuses every new account. Filling it in here would put one shared
  // secret into the image and into every checkout at once.
  //
  // Deployed it is a Container Apps secret, referenced rather than pasted, the way
  // the connection string is (#36). Step 15 of docs/deploy-azure.md has the
  // commands.
  //
  // There is nothing else here to configure. Accounts live in this application's
  // own database -- there is no identity provider, no client id and no redirect
  // URI to keep in step with anything.
  "Authentication": {
    "InviteCode": ""
  },

  // #88. Present and empty for the same reason InviteCode above is: these are the
  // two keys that exist, and a key nobody can find is a key nobody sets. Empty
  // reads as absent, which is the correct local answer -- the Data Protection key
  // ring then lives in memory and dies with the process, and on a machine its
  // owner restarts on purpose that is what should happen.
  //
  // Deployed they are set as ordinary environment variables and not as secrets:
  // they name a blob and a key, and reaching either needs the container app's
  // managed identity, which is not something a URL carries. Step 15 of
  // docs/deploy-azure.md has the commands, and ci.yml asserts both on every
  // deployment -- because with them unset the application starts, works, and signs
  // everybody out at the next cold start with nothing anywhere going red.
  //
  // Filling in one and not the other is refused at startup on purpose. The blob
  // alone would work, and would leave the key that decrypts every session cookie
  // in a container as readable XML.
  "DataProtection": {
    "KeyRingBlobUri": "",
    "KeyVaultKeyUri": ""
  },

  // The Python categorizer -- #39. Not a secret, so it lives in the committed
  // file rather than in user-secrets or a Container Apps secret: it names a
  // service, it carries no credential, and the three roads configuration takes
  // to this application (CLAUDE.md, "Configuration reaches the deployed app by
  // three roads") are about the connection string.
  //
  // **127.0.0.1, not localhost, and the difference is measured rather than
  // stylistic -- #59.** docker-compose publishes the categorizer on
  // `127.0.0.1:8000`, IPv4 only. On Windows the name `localhost` resolves to `::1`
  // first, nothing is listening there, and Docker Desktop swallows the attempt
  // instead of refusing it -- so the connection hangs for about two seconds before
  // falling back to IPv4. `.env.example` has carried that warning for the Postgres
  // port since before this key existed; this is the same trap, one service along.
  //
  // With `localhost` here and the two-second connect budget below, a save took the
  // **full eight seconds and stored no category**, against 156 ms once the name was
  // an address. So the dead IPv6 attempt was not merely slow, it consumed the whole
  // budget meant for a service that is not there. Padding the connect timeout past
  // it was the alternative and it hides the cause inside a number tuned to one
  // machine's name resolution.
  //
  // The app *container* overrides this with Categorizer__BaseUrl=http://categorizer:8000,
  // because inside the compose network localhost is the app's own container -- a
  // service name resolved by Docker's DNS, which has none of the above.
  //
  // **Deployed, this default is overridden too, and since #61 it must be.** The
  // categorizer is its own container app with internal ingress, so the deployed
  // value is Categorizer__BaseUrl=http://landmoney-categorizer.internal.<label>...
  // set on the app in Azure -- step 16 of docs/deploy-azure.md, asserted on every
  // deployment by ci.yml. Falling back to this line in production is the bug #61
  // was opened about: nothing listens on 127.0.0.1:8000 inside that container, so
  // every transaction is stored with no category and only the log says why.
  // Two timeouts since #59, and Program.cs carries the argument. The short
  // version: 2 s is how long to wait for a service that is not there -- #39
  // measured that a stopped categorizer leaves the SYN unanswered, so a save pays
  // this in full while it is down -- and 8 s is how long to wait for one that is
  // thinking, which a model needs and the rules never did. Both stay under the
  // browser client's REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS of 10 s.
  // #64. How often the one line saying what the categorizer did is written -- and
  // only when something happened, so an idle application writes nothing at all.
  // Set it to 0 to turn the summary off entirely, which is the right answer the day
  // something scrapes the meter instead; the per-call lines are unaffected either
  // way and are the durable record, because everything the summary counts lives in
  // this process and dies with it.
  // #93. BatchTimeoutSeconds is the overall budget for the one call a sweep tick
  // makes, and it is a second number rather than a larger first one. Eight seconds
  // is right for one row and wrong for a hundred, which at the Python side's
  // default concurrency is about twenty-six; raising the single number instead
  // would re-price #67's preview, which waits it out on the path where somebody is
  // typing. Nothing waits on this one -- it bounds a background sweep -- so the
  // cost of it being generous is a tick that runs long and a next tick that starts
  // late. ConnectTimeoutSeconds is deliberately shared: "the service is not there"
  // costs the same whichever call found out.
  //
  // #92. The sweep that categorises a row after it has been saved, rather than
  // during the request that saves it.
  //
  // SweepIntervalSeconds is most of what the wait feels like on screen: the client
  // polls while anything visible is uncategorised, so this is the gap between the
  // row appearing and its category appearing. Set it to 0 to turn categorising
  // after the fact off entirely -- rows still record that they are owed one and
  // are picked up whenever it is turned back on, so nothing is lost by it.
  //
  // MaxCategorizationAttempts is the ceiling on a bill rather than a tuning knob.
  // Every attempt that reaches the model is about 0.62 US cents (#87), so a row the
  // service keeps answering unusably would bill for ever without it; only outcomes
  // that could have cost something are charged against it, which is why an outage
  // does not exhaust it. Thirty at five seconds is two and a half minutes of
  // continuous failure before the oldest owed row is given up on -- and a row given
  // up on is still there, still uncategorised, and still labellable by hand.
  "Categorizer": {
    "BaseUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:8000",
    "TimeoutSeconds": 8,
    "BatchTimeoutSeconds": 60,
    "ConnectTimeoutSeconds": 2,
    "SummaryIntervalSeconds": 60,
    "SweepIntervalSeconds": 5,
    "SweepBatchSize": 20,
    "MaxCategorizationAttempts": 30
  }
}
